{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2d6bf00b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Linear Algebra and Probability – Wine Quality Dataset\n",
    "\n",
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e3d98df0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 1 – Load the dataset and handle missing data\n",
    "\n",
    "Load the Wine Quality dataset and replace null values with the mean of the respective column."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "ca53f318",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load the dataset\n",
    "df = pd.read_excel(\"Wine_Quality_Dataset.xlsx\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Dataset shape:\", df.shape)\n",
    "print(\"\\nMissing values before handling:\")\n",
    "print(df.isnull().sum())\n",
    "\n",
    "# Replace missing numerical values with the mean of their respective columns\n",
    "numeric_columns = df.select_dtypes(include=np.number).columns\n",
    "df[numeric_columns] = df[numeric_columns].fillna(df[numeric_columns].mean())\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nMissing values after handling:\")\n",
    "print(df.isnull().sum())\n",
    "\n",
    "df.head()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "845c1e49",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Answer\n",
    "\n",
    "The dataset contains **1599 rows and 12 columns**. There are **no missing/null values** in the supplied dataset, so no values needed to be replaced. The code nevertheless performs the required mean-imputation step, so it will also handle missing values if they are present."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "51e3f51a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 2 – Extract columns as vectors\n",
    "\n",
    "Extract the **alcohol** and **citric acid** columns as vectors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "86e5cfde",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Extract the requested columns as vectors\n",
    "alcohol_vector = df[\"alcohol\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "citric_acid_vector = df[\"citric acid\"].to_numpy()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Alcohol vector:\")\n",
    "print(alcohol_vector)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nCitric acid vector:\")\n",
    "print(citric_acid_vector)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nNumber of elements in alcohol vector:\", len(alcohol_vector))\n",
    "print(\"Number of elements in citric acid vector:\", len(citric_acid_vector))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "5393b51f",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Answer\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Alcohol vector:** contains the alcohol value for each of the 1599 wine samples.\n",
    "- **Citric acid vector:** contains the citric acid value for each of the 1599 wine samples.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, both vectors contain **1599 elements**."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "883b9bb5",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 3 – Calculate the covariance matrix\n",
    "\n",
    "Select **alcohol** and **density** as the two features and calculate their covariance matrix using `np.cov(X.T)`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "d45472e8",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Select alcohol and density\n",
    "X = df[[\"alcohol\", \"density\"]].to_numpy()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Calculate covariance matrix\n",
    "covariance_matrix = np.cov(X.T)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Feature matrix shape:\", X.shape)\n",
    "print(\"\\nCovariance matrix:\")\n",
    "print(covariance_matrix)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d2724b3c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Answer\n",
    "\n",
    "The covariance matrix for **alcohol** and **density** is approximately:\n",
    "\n",
    "\\[\n",
    "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
    "1.13564740 & -0.00099795 \\\\\n",
    "-0.00099795 & 0.000003562\n",
    "\\end{bmatrix}\n",
    "\\]\n",
    "\n",
    "The negative off-diagonal value indicates a **very small negative covariance** between alcohol and density in this dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "73d42d55",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 4 – Eigen decomposition\n",
    "\n",
    "Perform eigen decomposition on the covariance matrix. Identify the top two eigenvalues and their corresponding eigenvectors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "de8c731c",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Eigen decomposition\n",
    "eigenvalues, eigenvectors = np.linalg.eig(covariance_matrix)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Sort eigenvalues from largest to smallest\n",
    "order = np.argsort(eigenvalues)[::-1]\n",
    "eigenvalues = eigenvalues[order]\n",
    "eigenvectors = eigenvectors[:, order]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Eigenvalues:\")\n",
    "print(eigenvalues)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nEigenvectors (columns correspond to the eigenvalues):\")\n",
    "print(eigenvectors)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nTop 2 eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors:\")\n",
    "for i in range(2):\n",
    "    print(f\"Eigenvalue {i+1}: {eigenvalues[i]:.10f}\")\n",
    "    print(f\"Eigenvector {i+1}: {eigenvectors[:, i]}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3692d9bc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Answer and interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "The two eigenvalues are approximately:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Largest eigenvalue:** `1.13564827`\n",
    "   - Corresponding eigenvector ≈ **[0.999999614, -0.000878753]**\n",
    "\n",
    "2. **Second eigenvalue:** `0.00000268508`\n",
    "   - Corresponding eigenvector ≈ **[0.000878753, 0.999999614]**\n",
    "\n",
    "The first eigenvalue is much larger than the second. This means that almost all of the variance represented by this two-feature covariance matrix lies mainly along the first principal direction, which is strongly associated with **alcohol**. The second direction contributes very little variance."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "13df928e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Question 5 – Most common wine quality\n",
    "\n",
    "Find the most common wine quality score and interpret the distribution of quality scores."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "b51dd277",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Frequency of each wine quality score\n",
    "quality_counts = df[\"quality\"].value_counts().sort_index()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"Wine quality frequency:\")\n",
    "print(quality_counts)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Find the most common quality score\n",
    "most_common_quality = df[\"quality\"].mode()[0]\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nMost common wine quality:\", most_common_quality)\n",
    "print(\"Number of wines with this quality:\", quality_counts[most_common_quality])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nPercentage distribution:\")\n",
    "print((quality_counts / len(df) * 100).round(2))"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c5fdd803",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Answer\n",
    "\n",
    "The most common wine quality score is **5**, with **681 wines**.\n",
    "\n",
    "The quality scores range from **3 to 8**, but the majority of wines have quality scores of **5 or 6**. The distribution is concentrated around the middle quality levels rather than the extremes. In particular:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Quality 5: **681 wines**\n",
    "- Quality 6: **638 wines**\n",
    "- Quality 7: **199 wines**\n",
    "- Quality 4: **53 wines**\n",
    "- Quality 8: **18 wines**\n",
    "- Quality 3: **10 wines**\n",
    "\n",
    "Thus, the dataset contains mostly **medium-quality wines**, while very low and very high quality scores are relatively uncommon."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "8409407c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Final Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "| Question | Result |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Q1 | 1599 rows × 12 columns; no missing values in the supplied dataset |\n",
    "| Q2 | Extracted `alcohol` and `citric acid` as 1599-element vectors |\n",
    "| Q3 | Covariance calculated for `alcohol` and `density` |\n",
    "| Q4 | Top eigenvalues ≈ 1.13564827 and 0.00000268508 |\n",
    "| Q5 | Most common quality score is **5**, occurring 681 times |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Conclusion:** The Wine Quality dataset is suitable for demonstrating basic linear algebra and probability concepts. The covariance/eigenvalue analysis shows that the selected two-feature variation is dominated by alcohol, while the quality distribution is concentrated mainly at scores 5 and 6."
   ]
  }
